[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## Summary[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The bug was in [38;5;10mfips202.rs[0m in the [38;5;10mkeccak_f1600[0m function — the Keccak-f[1600] permutation, which is the core of SHAKE256.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Keccak permutation processes two rounds per loop iteration. In the [1msecond round[22m (the E→A direction), the C code uses temporary variables [38;5;10mBCa, BCe, BCi, BCo, BCu[0m to hold intermediate theta/rho/pi results before applying the chi step. These temporaries are independent of the state variables.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation incorrectly [1mreused A-state variables[22m ([38;5;10magi[0m, [38;5;10maga[0m, [38;5;10make[0m, [38;5;10mago[0m, [38;5;10maku[0m, [38;5;10mama[0m, etc.) as temporaries to hold intermediate E-state XOR values. The problem is that these A-state variables had [1malready been assigned their final values[22m in earlier rows of the same round's chi step. For example:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. Row 1 (chi step) computes [38;5;10magi = ...[0m (final value for [38;5;10mstate[7][0m)[0m[0m
2. Row 2 (theta/rho/pi step) overwrites [38;5;10magi = egi ^ di[0m (destroying the final value!)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This corrupted the Keccak state on every permutation call, producing wrong SHAKE256 outputs throughout the entire SPHINCS+ computation.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### What was changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Replaced the aliased A-state temporaries in round 2 with fresh local variables ([38;5;10mc_a[0m, [38;5;10mc_e[0m, [38;5;10mc_i[0m, [38;5;10mc_o[0m, [38;5;10mc_u[0m) for each of the 5 rows, matching the C code's approach of using separate [38;5;10mBCa[0m–[38;5;10mBCu[0m temporaries. Only [38;5;10mfips202.rs[0m was modified.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Why it didn't crash[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The sign-then-verify loop still "worked" because both signing and verification used the same (broken) SHAKE256, so they were self-consistent. But the outputs differed from the C reference implementation, causing the KAT transcript digest mismatch.[0m[0m
